---
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../consts';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { url } from '../url';
---

<Page
	title="About"
	description="What Primitive Bytes is about: data and AI governance, and building the platforms that make it real."
>
	<article class="wrap">
		<header class="head">
			<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
			<h1>I build governance into platforms. This blog wanders further.</h1>
		</header>
		<div class="prose">
			<p>
				Data and AI governance, and building the platforms that make it real, is what I do. Most
				organisations have the policy written down. Far fewer can enforce it at the moment data is
				read, a model is called or an agent acts, or show afterwards who allowed what. Closing that
				gap is the work.
			</p>
			<p>
				So a lot of what's here is about that. The running theme is <strong>primitives</strong>:
				small, versioned configs that describe one thing (a data contract, a tool lockfile, an
				agent manifest), are owned by someone, get reviewed like code, and are enforced by a tool
				rather than read by a person. The longer posts cover:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>data governance: contracts, catalogs, classification and access</li>
				<li>AI governance: what agents and models may touch, for whom, and the evidence after</li>
				<li>platform building: gateways, policy engines, and making the secure path the easy one</li>
				<li>agents and MCP: the protocols agents use, and where they fall short</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				But the blog isn't only that. The <strong>notes</strong> are shorter and about anything:
				whatever I'm building, reading, trying or thinking about that week, governance or not.
			</p>
			<p>
				I build in the open as I go. The first piece is <code>reef lock</code>, which pins every
				MCP tool's description and schemas so a server can't quietly change what an agent reads.
				It's in <a href="https://github.com/FatherBrian/trellis/tree/main/reef">Reef</a>, an
				open-source MCP gateway.
			</p>
			<p>
				Find me on <a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>, or follow along with the
				<a href={url('/rss.xml')}>RSS feed</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</article>
</Page>

<style>
	.head {
		max-width: var(--measure);
		margin: 4.5rem auto 2.5rem;
	}
	.head .eyebrow {
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
		color: var(--accent-ink);
	}
	@media (max-width: 640px) {
		.head {
			margin-top: 2.75rem;
		}
	}
</style>
